  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation sits on an explicit function with a body, `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` in `project_sources.rs`. That is a permitted target. The explanation is in fenced citation commentary, so it counts as tracked.\n\nThe claim matches the code. `outgoing_edges` in the `Project` fixture just returns `Ok(Vec::new())`, so this source-only fixture has no graph edges. A search of `project_sources.rs` finds no test there that calls `outgoing_edges`, which fits a recorded zero execution.\n\nThe claim that graph-selection regressions use other fixtures is also supported. Other test domains define real edge behavior and exercise it:\n- `Memory` in `agent/tests.rs#impl[Memory/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` returns a successor for node 1.\n- `GraphWithChangingEdges`, inside `agent/tests.rs#fn[agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets]`, has an `outgoing_edges` that changes between lookups.\n- `Memory` in `graph/validation_tests.rs#impl[Memory/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` records the selections it receives.\n- `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` asserts on edge selections.\n\nThe reason is substantive. It says the stub exists because the fixture has no edges, and it names where edge behavior is tested. It does not rest on difficulty. There is no redundant `{impl}` or `{test}` citation on the item. I found no gap.",
